<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khosrow II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/590">590</a>–<a href="/years/628">628 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khosrow II was the last great king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, reigning in the late sixth and early seventh centuries CE. His long war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> brought Sasanian armies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> before a Byzantine counter-offensive reversed these gains. He was overthrown and killed in 628, leaving an exhausted empire that soon fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii.jpg" alt="Khosrow II" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhosrauIIGoldCoinCroppedHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khosrow II was born about 570, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> and an unnamed noblewoman of the House of Ispahbudhan, one of the Seven Great Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Her brothers Vinduyih and Vistahm strongly influenced his early life. His grandfather was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>, and before his accession he served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> and was also at Partaw, the capital of Caucasian Albania, where he is said to have turned the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> into a Sasanian province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 590 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> disgraced and dismissed his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a>, who rebelled and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, declaring himself king of kings. Khosrow fled with his uncles, his wives and thirty nobles to Byzantine territory. In 591, with the support of the Byzantine emperor Maurice, he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and he was proclaimed king again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> in February. He showed tolerance toward his Christian subjects; his wife Shirin, a Christian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, had a church and monastery built near the palace at Ctesiphon. He later put his uncle Vinduyih to death, and in 600 he executed the Lakhmid king Al-Nu'man III, ending the Lakhmid buffer state.</p><p>When Maurice was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a> in 602, Khosrow began a war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, ostensibly to avenge him. Sasanian forces took Dara and Edessa in 605, plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and in 608 advanced into Chalcedon. Heraclius took the field in 622 and in 624 advanced into northern Adurbadagan, sacking cities and temples. After the capture of Dastagird, noble families and generals backed Khosrow's son Sheroe, who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> on the night of 25 February and imprisoned his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sheroe took the dynastic name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>, and Khosrow was killed. A civil war and interregnum followed. Khosrow's reign is regarded as a golden age of music, with the court musician Barbad, and he restored the erection of rock reliefs after nearly three centuries; the relief at Taq-e Bostan, left unfinished, shows his investiture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a>. He restored the title King of Kings to his coins. Persian poets later made him a hero, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>'s Khosrow and Shirin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khosrow II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It made possible Shapur I's capture of Antioch in the 250s, Shapur II's seizure of Amida (modern Diyarbakir) in 359 CE after a 73-day siege, and the fall of Jerusalem to <b>Khosrow II</b>'s general Shahrbaraz in 614 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In late Sasanian times a Pahlavi (Middle Persian) compilation, the Khwadāy-Nāmag or “Book of Kings”, told the history of the kings and heroes of Persia from mythical times down to the reign of <b>Khosrow II</b> (590–628).</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Shahrbaraz, a leading Sasanian general under <b>Khosrow II</b> who had captured Jerusalem from the Byzantines in 614, usurped the throne from Ardashir III.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-27">27 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Kavad II overthrew his father, <b>Khosrow II</b>, the last great king of the Sasanian Empire, after the defeat by Heraclius, and had him killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The murder gave the Sasanian king <b>Khosrow II</b> a pretext for a long war that exhausted both empires.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 602 <b>Khosrow II</b> deposed and executed al-Nu'man III, the last Lakhmid king, and replaced the dynasty with Persian governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmid Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=609" aria-label="Ctesiphon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Khosrow II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6033 4669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6033 4669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=609">What was here in 609 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
